"The giants are big and light: Jupiter has {#} Earth masses but a mean density of only {#} g/cm³, and Saturn, at {#} g/cm³, is less dense than water. They are mostly hydrogen and helium (Uranus and Neptune with more water, ammonia and methane), squeezed dense only near their centers.",
"Fast spin and low density flatten a planet: Saturn turns in {#} h and is {#} % wider at the equator than pole to pole.",
"A moon held together only by its own gravity is torn apart inside the Roche limit, about {#} R (ρ_planet/ρ_moon)^({#}/{#}) for a fluid moon. Saturn's main rings lie inside the limit for ice and its big moons outside. Denser moons can go closer.",
"Each ring particle is its own tiny moon, and inner ones go round faster (Kepler's third law), so a ring cannot be one solid sheet.",
"Moons weigh planets: a³/P² is the same for every moon of one planet and gives the planet's mass.",
"Io, Europa and Ganymede go round in a {#}:{#}:{#} resonance. The regular tugs keep Io's orbit oval, so Jupiter's tide on Io changes every orbit, and the flexing heats Io to the most volcanic world in the solar system. Enceladus and Dione ({#}:{#}) do the same on a smaller scale.",
"The strip across the top switches between four views, each flat or in 3D. <strong>Planets</strong> shows the four giants and Earth to scale, each at its axial tilt, with density bars against water. <strong>Rings</strong> looks down on a planet's rings to scale with the Roche limit for any moon density (fluid limit solid, rigid limit dashed) and a test moon, a pile of {#} balls held together only by gravity, at any distance from {#} to {#} planet radii; drag it in or out. Paint a line across the rings to watch it shear; show the Mimas {#}:{#} place and the ring moons that shepherd and clear gaps. <strong>Moons</strong> shows the main moons on their Kepler ellipses from their J2000 places (Jupiter and Saturn; Uranus's and Neptune's starting places are spread out for clarity), drawn {#} times larger than life and colored by density, with the lines where resonant pairs pass each other; for Jupiter, Moons pull on each other computes the four big moons step by step instead and graphs Io's eccentricity. <strong>Io</strong> follows Io on its oval orbit (eccentricity {#} to {#}, drawn up to {#} times more oval) with a close up of the tidal bulge and its tidal heating. Sixteen setups, from Four giants to scale to Io on a circle. The test moon takes about {#} seconds of real time for three orbits.",
