"<strong>Io's tides heat it</strong>, then <strong>Io on a circle</strong>. Ask Q5.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Saturn has {#} times Earth's mass and {#} times Earth's volume. What is its mean density, and what does it tell us about what Saturn is made of? Why is Saturn the flattest planet?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> Saturn's A ring ends at {#} Saturn radii. The demo puts the Roche limit at {#} radii for a moon of solid ice ({#} g/cm³) and {#} radii for porous ice ({#} g/cm³). Mimas orbits at {#} radii. Why are there rings close in and moons farther out? The demo's ice moon, a pile of boulders, survives at {#} radii but comes apart at {#}. Why does it survive inside the drawn limit?",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> By Kepler's third law a particle at the inner edge of the C ring goes round in {#} h and one at the outer edge of the A ring in {#} h. How long does it take the inner one to gain a whole lap on the outer one? What does that mean for a ring made as a single solid sheet?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> Io goes round Jupiter in {#} days, Europa in {#} days and Ganymede in {#} days. Show that Io passes Europa every {#} days and Europa passes Ganymede every {#} days, twice as long. Why can all three never line up on the same side of Jupiter?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> The demo gives Io {#} × {#}¹³ W of tidal heating at its real eccentricity, {#}, and none on a circle. Compare that with Earth's whole internal heat flow, {#} TW, and explain why the heating would stop if Europa and Ganymede were taken away.",
"<strong>\"Saturn's rings are solid.\"</strong> They are countless separate pieces of ice, from dust to house sized, each on its own orbit; the inner ones go round faster.",
"<strong>\"Gas giants are made of gas all the way through.\"</strong> Deep inside, the pressure turns hydrogen liquid and then metallic, and the centers are far denser than water. The mean density is low because most of the volume is the light outer layers.",
"<strong>\"The Roche limit applies to everything, including spacecraft and people.\"</strong> It applies to bodies held together by their own gravity. A spacecraft or a small rock with strength is held by chemical bonds, far stronger than its gravity, and is not torn apart.",
"<strong>\"Tidal heating comes from the tide itself.\"</strong> A steady tide stretches a moon once and stops. Heating needs the tide to change, which needs an oval orbit (or a moon not turning in step).",
"<strong>\"Jupiter is almost a star.\"</strong> It would need about {#} times its mass to fuse hydrogen.",
"Planet data are the NASA planetary fact sheets (NSSDCA, D. Williams): masses, GM, equatorial, polar and mean radii, flattening, rotation periods, tilts, J₂. Moon distances, periods, eccentricities, radii and densities are from the NSSDCA satellite fact sheets and JPL's satellite mean elements; the J2000 places of Jupiter's and Saturn's moons are JPL mean longitudes and longitudes of periapsis (node + argument of periapsis + mean anomaly) for noon on {#} January {#}. Inclinations, precession and the moons' pulls on each other are left out of the Kepler ellipses, so the places drift from the real ones over years. Nereid (e = {#}) is left out.",
"Roche limits: d = {#} R (ρ_P/ρ)^({#}/{#}) for a fluid moon, from Chandrasekhar's critical value GM/d³ = {#} πGρ (Ellipsoidal Figures of Equilibrium, {#}, ch. {#}); d = {#}^({#}/{#}) R (ρ_P/ρ)^({#}/{#}) = {#} R (ρ_P/ρ)^({#}/{#}) for a loose rock on a rigid round moon turning once per orbit. The unit R (ρ_P/ρ)^({#}/{#}) is computed from the planet's mass, so it does not depend on which radius is used.",
"The test moon is {#} equal frictionless balls with soft contact springs and restitution damping that attract each other by gravity, settled into a pile and then placed in Hill's frame (Coriolis, centrifugal and tidal terms to first order in the moon's size). Whether such a pile survives depends only on its distance in units of R (ρ_P/ρ)^({#}/{#}): it holds for at least {#} orbits at {#} and comes apart within {#} orbits at {#} or closer (curriculum/validation/test_giant-planets.cjs). Its density is taken inside its outer surface.",
"Rings: NASA ring radii; brightness in the picture is a drawing choice. Ring particles go round at Kepler speed √(GM/r³); the bulge changes the periods by under {#} %. The Mimas {#}:{#} place solves {#}Ω − κ = 2n_Mimas with J₂ ({#} {#} km, beside the B ring's outer edge at {#} {#} km; Kepler's law alone gives {#} {#} km).",
"Io's tidal heating: Ė = ({#}/{#})(k₂/Q) G M_J² R⁵ n e² / a⁶ (Murray and Dermott, Solar System Dynamics, {#}, eq. {#}), with k₂/Q = {#} ± {#} from Io's measured orbit (Lainey and others, Nature {#}, {#}, {#}). The measured heat flow is at least {#} W/m² (Veeder and others, JGR {#}, {#}, {#}). Earth's {#} TW is from Davies and Davies (Solid Earth {#}, {#}, {#}).",
"Moons pull on each other (Jupiter only): Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto in Jupiter's equator plane, each pulled by Jupiter, by Jupiter's J₂ and by the other three (with the indirect terms of a Jupiter centered frame), stepped by leapfrog every {#} s from circular orbits at their J2000 longitudes. GM values are JPL's ({#}, {#}, {#} and {#} km³/s²). Io's eccentricity is the osculating value with J₂ in the central pull. Energy holds to {#} parts in a million over {#} days and Io's period comes out at {#} days. Europa forces e ≈ {#} on Io, half the peak (the measured value is {#}, JPL mean elements); starting on a circle adds an equal free wobble, so e swings from {#} to {#} and back over about {#} days, the time for Io and Europa's meeting place to come back round. Without Europa, e stays below {#} (curriculum/validation/test_giant-planets.cjs).",
"Flattening: a uniform planet would take f = 5q/{#} for its spin (q = ω²a³/GM); every giant bulges less, because its mass is packed toward the center.",
"Pair with <strong>Tides</strong> for the tidal stretch on Earth and Moon, with <strong>Kepler Orbits</strong> for Kepler's third law around the Sun, and with <strong>Orbital Resonances</strong> for the same kind of resonance in the asteroid belt (the Kirkwood gaps) and the Kuiper belt (Pluto and Neptune).",
